Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Parameter invariance is gauge invariance \
for a one-dimensional world line",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ".\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975109729054944*^9, 3.975109735320888*^9}, {
  3.975109766020455*^9, 3.975109769090336*^9}, {3.9751554322535677`*^9, 
  3.975155435689021*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"481c7e62-0939-4628-ad20-\
3ac6be966817"],

Cell["\<\
My job in this notebook is to make that slogan clear, then to bridge it to a \
lot more physics.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975113412224842*^9, 3.97511342282714*^9}, {
  3.975113832485095*^9, 
  3.97511384957141*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9aa699aa-53ae-43a0-995d-\
4671e70ea3c3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975106336405767*^9, 
  3.975106338109687*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ee9b2c23-b4ea-4085-ab93-\
d72b29aac5f0"],

Cell["\<\
To understand nothing about the Standard Model of particle physics would be a \
shame, akin to never seeing Michelangelo\[CloseCurlyQuote]s art or to never \
hearing Bach\[CloseCurlyQuote]s music. The Standard Model is one of the \
greatest intellectual achievements of all time, and it also is one of the two \
most spectacularly verified theories in all of science, along with General \
Relativity. Nothing else even comes close to those two. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975106357905342*^9, 3.9751069321986237`*^9}, {
  3.975107548322527*^9, 3.975107573341488*^9}, {3.97510815016485*^9, 
  3.975108164608101*^9}, {3.975110184734994*^9, 3.975110186791842*^9}, {
  3.975110321732748*^9, 3.975110331212584*^9}, {3.975155468316078*^9, 
  3.9751555113082952`*^9}, {3.975165364464201*^9, 3.9751653935466337`*^9}, {
  3.97523314535644*^9, 3.975233155869842*^9}, {3.975233390175385*^9, 
  3.975233413151894*^9}, {3.9752334514074183`*^9, 3.975233451955357*^9}, {
  3.975233483067791*^9, 
  3.975233514171049*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d31de278-99dd-4046-8e53-\
3a7e5470e1d7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It is sobering to realize that we ",
 StyleBox["really do know just how things work",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ": from quarks and gluons, electrons and photons, atoms and molecules, all \
the way to superclusters of galaxies. Even more sobering, the same kind of \
concise mathematics pertains to all of it, starting with the magical \
Lagrangian. Its depths reveal curvatures and invariants of abstract \
geometrical spaces that drive everything we know about the Universe. In this \
notebook, we hope to show that if you know just a bit of Classical Mechanics \
at a University level, you can get into the Standard Model to some depth. \
Both the Standard Model and General Relativity flow naturally from a magical \
reformulation of plain-old Newtonian mechanics, just ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"F", "=", 
    RowBox[{"m", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "a"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"fca0a9de-18ce-46be-8e33-2cf97418fb4a"],
 ". "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975106357905342*^9, 3.9751069321986237`*^9}, {
  3.975107548322527*^9, 3.975107573341488*^9}, {3.97510815016485*^9, 
  3.975108164608101*^9}, {3.975110184734994*^9, 3.975110186791842*^9}, {
  3.975110321732748*^9, 3.975110331212584*^9}, {3.975155468316078*^9, 
  3.9751555113082952`*^9}, {3.975165364464201*^9, 3.9751653935466337`*^9}, {
  3.97523314535644*^9, 3.975233155869842*^9}, {3.975233390175385*^9, 
  3.975233413151894*^9}, {3.9752334514074183`*^9, 3.975233451955357*^9}, {
  3.975233483067791*^9, 3.975233505654842*^9}, {3.97526090748949*^9, 
  3.975260946403995*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6f61707a-05fe-4739-b85a-\
ee00073ec7f8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s assume you are acquainted, even just superficially, \
with the Lagrangian and Hamiltonian approaches to Classical Mechanics, say \
from a course in ",
 ButtonBox["Goldstein's book",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://www.amazon.com/Classical-Mechanics-3rd-Herbert-Goldstein/dp/\
0201657023/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3UCWL1E5GKIIQ&dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.\
p5kPQfqj1poHbtKZsN0UTEYdLBf_9vleD94bKeaOPn64PsNlEAeN5pG-\
Bm2lyK3aMITXYFeelZhqoQIsLV4eny68OTv6o--\
kHUgxHgqlOgdH1KOKiYQcoKK5lEWO04YYw1zqziAzCv4U8lcm7M4o3P1Klf6XYSSzbnenTnXd3-\
iFK4OgkGmUfOfPQvA4Jsx4Ttq8CPcLEERe8AZ2oHxQajN2CefyYlGiJXUs9vpzdzs.\
cmTH4POCuVkgMdw2eUvs6bq-ed-iP2Zkiwx-5yQL6zU&dib_tag=se&keywords=goldstein+\
classical+mechanics&qid=1766271652&sprefix=goldstein+%2Caps%2C193&sr=8-1"], 
    None},
  ButtonNote->
   "https://www.amazon.com/Classical-Mechanics-3rd-Herbert-Goldstein/dp/\
0201657023/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3UCWL1E5GKIIQ&dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.\
p5kPQfqj1poHbtKZsN0UTEYdLBf_9vleD94bKeaOPn64PsNlEAeN5pG-\
Bm2lyK3aMITXYFeelZhqoQIsLV4eny68OTv6o--\
kHUgxHgqlOgdH1KOKiYQcoKK5lEWO04YYw1zqziAzCv4U8lcm7M4o3P1Klf6XYSSzbnenTnXd3-\
iFK4OgkGmUfOfPQvA4Jsx4Ttq8CPcLEERe8AZ2oHxQajN2CefyYlGiJXUs9vpzdzs.\
cmTH4POCuVkgMdw2eUvs6bq-ed-iP2Zkiwx-5yQL6zU&dib_tag=se&keywords=goldstein+\
classical+mechanics&qid=1766271652&sprefix=goldstein+%2Caps%2C193&sr=8-1"],
 ". If you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve seen that a derivative of kinetic energy ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["1", "2"], "m", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    SuperscriptBox["v", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7aaa8e5f-efcf-4420-b13f-8fac4dc3035c"],
 " with respect to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["v", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "903a14f3-6cf1-4766-a3fa-20f18006e0e2"],
 " gives ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"m", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "v"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "11b57cad-b4b3-41ae-8cb7-a6006f595c6e"],
 ", which equals momentum, that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s probably enough to cross \
my bridge. I have posted several notebooks to the Wolfram Community with \
examples of the Lagrangian approach at work on the symmetrical spinning top \
and other scenarios. You should know a bit of multivariable calculus, too. I \
do ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " assume you\[CloseCurlyQuote]re acquainted with tensors, differential \
geometry, or gauge theory. My job is to introduce you to these topics, \
showing how they follow directly from the Lagrangian approach. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97510586447761*^9, 3.97510611954949*^9}, {
   3.975106170896216*^9, 3.97510620376901*^9}, {3.97511356723127*^9, 
   3.9751135693053427`*^9}, 3.975113991519876*^9, {3.975155637478589*^9, 
   3.975155776628056*^9}, {3.9751558362006693`*^9, 3.975155858306175*^9}, {
   3.9751654161954107`*^9, 3.975165436932131*^9}, {3.975183075204752*^9, 
   3.975183099833302*^9}, {3.975233562796393*^9, 3.975233576772748*^9}, {
   3.975240045630756*^9, 3.975240109274549*^9}, {3.975256962339995*^9, 
   3.975256988009602*^9}, {3.975260975513341*^9, 3.9752610210023413`*^9}, {
   3.9752845849266443`*^9, 
   3.9752845854273443`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f3b595a6-6b5c-44a5-9c1f-\
363bfc637819"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "There\[CloseCurlyQuote]s no novel theory here, only newly concise and clear \
explanations, a hitch-hiker\[CloseCurlyQuote]s guide to the staggeringly \
large and difficult literature of contemporary physics. I move you quickly \
from basic mechanics to the frontiers of quantum fields. All we need is the \
practical realization that ",
 StyleBox["the labels we choose for points in any kind of space should not \
change the laws of physics",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". I leave vast and steep abstractions to others, not to denigrate their \
value, but to cut to the chase."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97510739287677*^9, 3.975107439904378*^9}, {
  3.9751074708878593`*^9, 3.975107540884181*^9}, {3.975107589966318*^9, 
  3.97510767663393*^9}, {3.9751081818297167`*^9, 3.975108214146472*^9}, {
  3.975108326476943*^9, 3.975108352578435*^9}, {3.975110286092692*^9, 
  3.975110290170251*^9}, {3.975113597754655*^9, 3.975113625000752*^9}, {
  3.9751558743271027`*^9, 3.975155978989197*^9}, {3.975156032206976*^9, 
  3.975156035461288*^9}, {3.975240133921575*^9, 3.9752401787721987`*^9}, {
  3.9752402409835978`*^9, 3.975240366097034*^9}, {3.9752610795150023`*^9, 
  3.975261150322404*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c2e54895-6cb4-4db9-ae7b-\
6f71ef6d7a0f"],

Cell["By the end of this notebook, you should be able to", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97510586447761*^9, 3.97510611954949*^9}, {
  3.975106170896216*^9, 3.9751061891593637`*^9}, {3.9751082588218718`*^9, 
  3.975108262061102*^9}, {3.9752403758152523`*^9, 
  3.9752403837046967`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dbb2c3fe-b430-4093-85f5-\
6269125364ea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Demonstrate the Tensorial Nature of Motion",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": show that the Euler-Lagrange equations are a ",
 StyleBox["covariant covector",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", a purely geometrical object that fully captures the dynamics of a system \
across arbitrary definitions of coordinates and time. "
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975106232667241*^9, 3.975106317386038*^9}, {
   3.9751069703635883`*^9, 3.9751069946456842`*^9}, {3.975108274385585*^9, 
   3.9751082918843517`*^9}, 3.9751102033353233`*^9, {3.9751560467642603`*^9, 
   3.9751560560978947`*^9}, {3.97515609049944*^9, 3.975156093074419*^9}, {
   3.975240406223764*^9, 3.9752404082635117`*^9}, {3.975261181172365*^9, 
   3.975261194469172*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"57aa87ed-708d-472f-a8bc-\
73e953971c60"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Identify the Geometric Signature of Relativity",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": explain why requiring a theory to be invariant with respect to choice of \
a time-evolution parameter forces a Lagrangian to be ",
 StyleBox["homogeneous of degree 1",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975107007547861*^9, 3.975107076431363*^9}, {
  3.975107167089424*^9, 3.975107178319149*^9}, {3.975108480837728*^9, 
  3.975108488936694*^9}, {3.975113666355583*^9, 3.975113707094315*^9}, {
  3.975156113695263*^9, 3.975156115252096*^9}, {3.975240431701672*^9, 
  3.975240438546393*^9}, {3.9752612141816053`*^9, 
  3.975261216281542*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a8ebf704-ae5d-4708-8b2a-\
4a797162976f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Diagnose \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Singular Systems\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": show that such homogeneity forces a ",
 StyleBox["singular Hessian matrix",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", preventing us from solving for momenta in terms of velocities. "
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975107081010543*^9, 3.975107229285177*^9}, 
   3.9751074623793697`*^9, {3.975108364715611*^9, 3.975108396012751*^9}, {
   3.9751137202631903`*^9, 3.9751137206111727`*^9}, {3.97524046501204*^9, 
   3.975240518786746*^9}, {3.975261225558463*^9, 
   3.9752612259760857`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fcc39b76-91f3-4a7a-a56e-\
d37ee5010ace"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Invariance Implies Constants of Motion",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": ",
 "Derive both the ",
 StyleBox["relativistic mass-shell condition",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 StyleBox[", ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["p", "2"], "=", 
    SuperscriptBox["m", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FontWeight->"Bold",ExpressionUUID->"011f9836-ae96-4ad7-acf5-04293537d5a6"],
 ", and ",
 StyleBox["the vanishing Hamiltonian",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " as direct consequences of parameter-invariance."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975107081010543*^9, 3.975107263236245*^9}, {
   3.975107301006054*^9, 3.975107384779079*^9}, {3.9751084102227087`*^9, 
   3.9751084695474586`*^9}, {3.975113737729883*^9, 3.9751137734980383`*^9}, 
   3.975156139616222*^9, {3.975240557068939*^9, 3.975240636056366*^9}, {
   3.975248820888897*^9, 
   3.9752488496063223`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8bab6759-32c4-49bb-a785-\
a54b015618fe"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Anticipate Relativistic Quantum Fields",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": be confident that we can extend these ideas from classical mechanics \
through to advanced physics, affording deeply rooted understanding from first \
principles."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97510739287677*^9, 3.975107439904378*^9}, {
  3.9751074708878593`*^9, 3.975107540884181*^9}, {3.975107586014166*^9, 
  3.9751075860158052`*^9}, {3.97510822459092*^9, 3.975108226011159*^9}, {
  3.975113784509482*^9, 3.9751137870558662`*^9}, {3.975156167808625*^9, 
  3.975156226393532*^9}, {3.9752406609062843`*^9, 3.975240704101302*^9}, {
  3.9752612574781923`*^9, 
  3.9752612593678627`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"46fad1c5-74be-4568-a62a-\
7b852c5b3464"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975106126052712*^9, 
  3.975106127761718*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a31e6198-3c42-4c6a-960b-\
3b733ca0e9cb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Actual physical motion should not depend on choice of coordinate system, \
within reason. Cartesian coordinates can be freely repositioned and rotated, \
yet still describe the same physics. Spherical and cylindrical coordinates \
routinely simplify problems. Classical textbooks like ",
 ButtonBox["Morse and Feshbach",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://a.co/d/hwkM9Wb"], None},
  ButtonNote->"https://a.co/d/hwkM9Wb"],
 " describe many more useful coordinate systems, some highly esoteric and \
specialized. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975103219685441*^9, 3.975103288725281*^9}, {
   3.975103359960136*^9, 3.9751035164288797`*^9}, {3.9751035801644173`*^9, 
   3.975103614268771*^9}, {3.975104278891995*^9, 3.97510428148212*^9}, 
   3.975112337871217*^9, {3.9751138006607523`*^9, 3.975113814858872*^9}, {
   3.975179490537754*^9, 3.97517949606735*^9}, {3.975240720238357*^9, 
   3.9752408072270393`*^9}, {3.97526129157365*^9, 
   3.9752612945470257`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"56e204e9-8d6b-4858-8740-\
2999aefdf679"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Coordinate independence has its origin in \
cartography, with the effort to fit a spherical Earth onto a flat piece of \
mapping paper under this-or-that constraint: equal area, equal angles, \
straight lines for geodesics, and so on.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975261324391843*^9, 
  3.975261425495261*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0da00ac5-8789-4699-93ff-\
3f76ae1e5c0c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Lagrangian mechanics takes coordinate independence to the max: we abstract ",
 StyleBox["any",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " measurable quantities that constrain the ",
 StyleBox["kinematics",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of a system\[LongDash]the ways it ",
 StyleBox["can",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " move\[LongDash]into ",
 StyleBox["generalized coordinates",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". Angle of a wheel, length of a spring or a pulley cord, displacement of a \
joint on a sliding rod, and so on. Generalized coordinates, when chosen to \
honor constraints, eliminate accounting for reaction forces, making seemingly \
impossible problems tractable. Imagine describing the motion of the pistons, \
cranks, linkages, governors, and wheels of a steam locomotive purely via \
Newton\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Laws in Cartesian coordinates. This same problem \
melts away and becomes even fun via the Lagrangian approach."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975103219685441*^9, 3.975103288725281*^9}, {
   3.975103359960136*^9, 3.9751035164288797`*^9}, {3.9751035801644173`*^9, 
   3.9751039183513613`*^9}, {3.975104035066607*^9, 3.9751041851499033`*^9}, {
   3.9751047020530243`*^9, 3.9751047340009527`*^9}, {3.9751139115928907`*^9, 
   3.975113928183037*^9}, {3.97515628998873*^9, 3.975156345145455*^9}, {
   3.975179527926496*^9, 3.975179560110668*^9}, {3.9751796826830378`*^9, 
   3.9751797069137774`*^9}, 3.975240856861504*^9, {3.975248895265161*^9, 
   3.975248939418943*^9}, {3.975261399655921*^9, 3.975261404166314*^9}, {
   3.975261453169374*^9, 
   3.975261459020898*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4e7ceb9c-d0d7-451f-be88-\
246b12ce8480"],

Cell["\<\
There really is no price to pay for the Lagrangian approach. The benefits \
compound as we progress from \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]homework problems\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] into relativity, field theory, quantum mechanics, \
and all the way to the Standard Model and General Relativity. But how?\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975103937305183*^9, 3.9751040142863626`*^9}, {
  3.975104206880567*^9, 3.975104208614902*^9}, {3.97510585079037*^9, 
  3.9751058585994663`*^9}, {3.975108564720406*^9, 3.9751085698198643`*^9}, {
  3.975156367332013*^9, 3.9751563702861423`*^9}, {3.97524086774175*^9, 
  3.975240885333048*^9}, {3.975261490110989*^9, 
  3.975261491367703*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2de76ad9-10e2-4066-be15-\
f8d56e0fa3ba"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The generalized coordinates for a specific problem actually describe a \
miniature Universe, a ",
 StyleBox["configuration manifold",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", with its own idiosyncratic geometry and topology, its own curvatures and \
invariants. This fact unlocks the secret ",
 StyleBox["geometric",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " power of the Lagrangian approach. Configuration ",
 "manifolds",
 " succumb to the mathematics of ",
 StyleBox["differential geometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975104212596513*^9, 3.975104220380477*^9}, {
   3.9751043174045553`*^9, 3.975104682058918*^9}, {3.975104758518982*^9, 
   3.975104986843252*^9}, {3.9751058231613503`*^9, 3.9751058303769484`*^9}, {
   3.975108600358955*^9, 3.975108664836718*^9}, {3.9751087337489433`*^9, 
   3.9751088751849527`*^9}, {3.9751089065866756`*^9, 3.975109046185907*^9}, {
   3.975109300209927*^9, 3.975109417329453*^9}, 3.975112515793837*^9, {
   3.975112570944889*^9, 3.9751125752615633`*^9}, {3.9751126966068363`*^9, 
   3.975112769525168*^9}, 3.975113972757415*^9, {3.975114009018289*^9, 
   3.975114095203519*^9}, {3.975156387575515*^9, 3.975156418766995*^9}, {
   3.975166379926908*^9, 3.975166408714355*^9}, {3.9751795869187098`*^9, 
   3.975179603924993*^9}, {3.975181321241921*^9, 3.975181322604624*^9}, {
   3.97526151659536*^9, 
   3.975261524404894*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d54111f1-6a93-4b3a-9f53-\
9cba126f4ed2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Even from popular literature, we recognize ",
 StyleBox["differential geometry",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " as the mathematics of cosmological gravitation via space-time curvature, \
and so it is. But the same framework also pertains to Lagrangian \
configuration manifolds. There we find curvatures and geodesics that \
constrain the ",
 StyleBox["dynamics",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of a system as it evolves and moves, analogous to the curvatures and \
geodesics that constrain gravitational motion in General Relativity. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975104212596513*^9, 3.975104220380477*^9}, {
   3.9751043174045553`*^9, 3.975104682058918*^9}, {3.975104758518982*^9, 
   3.975104986843252*^9}, {3.9751058231613503`*^9, 3.9751058303769484`*^9}, {
   3.975108600358955*^9, 3.975108664836718*^9}, {3.9751087337489433`*^9, 
   3.9751088751849527`*^9}, {3.9751089065866756`*^9, 3.975109046185907*^9}, {
   3.975109300209927*^9, 3.975109417329453*^9}, 3.975112515793837*^9, {
   3.975112570944889*^9, 3.9751125752615633`*^9}, {3.9751126966068363`*^9, 
   3.975112769525168*^9}, 3.975113972757415*^9, {3.975114009018289*^9, 
   3.975114095203519*^9}, {3.975156387575515*^9, 3.975156503050631*^9}, {
   3.975156612114314*^9, 3.975156615026059*^9}, {3.975166051078123*^9, 
   3.975166114729218*^9}, {3.975178952066585*^9, 3.975178970009842*^9}, {
   3.975179633086795*^9, 3.97517966556918*^9}, {3.975181365868148*^9, 
   3.97518139560859*^9}, {3.975240925184361*^9, 3.975240962997787*^9}, {
   3.975248950463756*^9, 
   3.975248969929439*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9110cec1-1428-4222-9bd2-\
457eae9931aa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Another slogan: ",
 StyleBox["kinematics by choice of coordinates, dynamics by intrinsic \
geometry.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975240990734384*^9, 3.9752410555838547`*^9}, {
  3.975241160109151*^9, 3.97524116590513*^9}, {3.9752615615793753`*^9, 
  3.975261564018632*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"195a1f04-34c1-4902-9b06-\
a5f311bee4c9"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Intrinsic\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] here means precisely those facts of geometry that do \
not depend on choice of coordinates.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975261578162198*^9, 
  3.975261603274836*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"60e494dd-8f9d-4e80-ba19-\
5222036e42fe"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Pressing on, we discover ",
 StyleBox["coordinate invariants",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ": integrals that do not depend on choice of coordinates. As a system \
evolves dynamically, its state, a sequence of coordinate values, traces out a \
",
 StyleBox["world line",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", a curved path through the configuration manifold. The coordinate \
invariants are properties that remain \[OpenCurlyQuote]true\[CloseCurlyQuote] \
of the world line regardless of coordinate system. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975104212596513*^9, 3.975104220380477*^9}, {
   3.9751043174045553`*^9, 3.975104682058918*^9}, {3.975104758518982*^9, 
   3.975104986843252*^9}, {3.9751058231613503`*^9, 3.9751058303769484`*^9}, {
   3.975108600358955*^9, 3.975108664836718*^9}, {3.9751087337489433`*^9, 
   3.9751088751849527`*^9}, {3.9751089065866756`*^9, 3.975109046185907*^9}, {
   3.975109300209927*^9, 3.9751094727859507`*^9}, {3.9751098432416553`*^9, 
   3.975109933698944*^9}, {3.9751141077388897`*^9, 3.975114109946827*^9}, 
   3.975114144587685*^9, {3.9751565161408243`*^9, 3.975156585398512*^9}, {
   3.975156642438039*^9, 3.975156675240803*^9}, {3.975156733155343*^9, 
   3.975156735703326*^9}, {3.9751567901382923`*^9, 3.9751567901961937`*^9}, {
   3.975178537419321*^9, 3.9751785458069763`*^9}, {3.975178856259397*^9, 
   3.975178924426078*^9}, {3.975178994887495*^9, 3.975179051418928*^9}, {
   3.9751814125993013`*^9, 3.9751814282151623`*^9}, {3.97524106849135*^9, 
   3.975241075090106*^9}, {3.97524117800106*^9, 3.97524118343552*^9}, {
   3.975261646551168*^9, 
   3.975261647609847*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b67f379f-9ced-4bc1-b8c3-\
4003f9efab4f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Naturally, we first think of Newtonian time for parametrizing dynamical \
progress along the world line. But suppose we ",
 StyleBox["further",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " require that the Lagrangian not even depend on how we measure dynamical \
progress (within reason)\[LongDash]on what \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]clock\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] we choose. Relativity pops out of a ",
 StyleBox["parameter-invariant",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " Lagrangian, almost without effort. Old Newtonian time becomes just another \
coordinate on the manifold, rather than a privileged background parameter. \
The parameter that measures progress\[LongDash]the clock\[LongDash]becomes \
nearly arbitrary. In fact, it becomes a freely chosen ",
 StyleBox["gauge",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975104815958149*^9, 3.975104819604298*^9}, {
   3.9751049396354094`*^9, 3.975104943206053*^9}, {3.975105018474543*^9, 
   3.975105155727556*^9}, 3.9751051970360947`*^9, {3.9751090717231817`*^9, 
   3.9751090909619303`*^9}, {3.975109874540559*^9, 3.97510988232299*^9}, {
   3.975109965534247*^9, 3.975110003284951*^9}, {3.97511417650778*^9, 
   3.975114283174782*^9}, {3.9751143179882183`*^9, 3.9751143656395073`*^9}, {
   3.975114460188774*^9, 3.975114480845272*^9}, {3.975114619953948*^9, 
   3.975114668946405*^9}, {3.9751566847059393`*^9, 3.975156716329008*^9}, {
   3.9751666936982927`*^9, 3.975166696738003*^9}, {3.9751783989464073`*^9, 
   3.9751784009045677`*^9}, {3.97517909660561*^9, 3.9751792520193653`*^9}, {
   3.975181440930257*^9, 3.9751815722963533`*^9}, {3.9752490420015173`*^9, 
   3.9752491891591988`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5e50fb17-68ac-417e-8975-\
a60c189e6999"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So, by combining coordinate invariance with parameter invariance, we \
discover ",
 StyleBox["gauge invariance",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", the gateway to advanced physics. ",
 StyleBox["Parameter invariance is gauge invariance for a one-dimensional \
world line",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". It is the first glimmer of further, wildly general gauge invariants that \
constitute the Standard Model."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9751051605319*^9, 3.975105272589864*^9}, {
   3.975109127162195*^9, 3.9751092026885977`*^9}, 3.9751099520452347`*^9, {
   3.975114488487721*^9, 3.9751144969891033`*^9}, {3.9751145805416107`*^9, 
   3.97511458573147*^9}, {3.975179299329357*^9, 3.9751793438866453`*^9}, {
   3.975181596254141*^9, 3.975181599657558*^9}, {3.9752491977299757`*^9, 
   3.975249255658514*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"05394a78-fa2f-4e64-8313-\
43e90bb469a7"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["The term \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]gauge\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] has its origin again in the steam age, where it \
refers to an arbitrary choice of the width of a rail line.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975261688130226*^9, 3.9752617583853416`*^9}, {
  3.975262398278481*^9, 
  3.975262408439684*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"21ff07cd-de7a-4777-8dcc-\
609b20bcfce4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this notebook, we unlock the gateway and peek beyond. We\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll derive Einstein\[CloseCurlyQuote]s famous formula ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["p", "2"], "=", 
    SuperscriptBox["m", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6c58e712-11b5-4625-b83d-a48e93ac1530"],
 " (aka \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"E", "=", 
    RowBox[{"m", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
     SuperscriptBox["c", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a3b7bed8-2434-40a5-9f8a-5385e484af58"],
 ",\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] imprecisely) as an ",
 StyleBox["algebraic",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " consequence of the unsolvability of Hamiltonian momenta in terms of \
coordinate velocities. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll end with a roadmap to the more \
general gauge theories of relativistic quantum fields by exactly the same \
procedures."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975105277151897*^9, 3.975105530778509*^9}, {
  3.975166739747957*^9, 3.975166782481904*^9}, {3.97516682555159*^9, 
  3.975166845624613*^9}, {3.975179379859398*^9, 3.975179464827705*^9}, {
  3.975181624161442*^9, 3.9751816306332483`*^9}, {3.975190056002894*^9, 
  3.9751900860547447`*^9}, {3.975249269860272*^9, 
  3.975249282588978*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"537f7107-297f-450f-adc4-\
44d846152827"]
}, Open  ]],

